// The Pico Breadboard Kit — a program, not a package.
//
// Nothing imports this. It is the *board glue* for one carrier board: the pin map, the SPI and GPIO
// bring-up, and a demo that shows the parts working. `sh.sysl.st7796`'s README says that glue
// "belongs in the program, not here", so that the driver does not quietly become a package for one
// board — and this is the program it means.

package {
  name    = "picokit"
  version = "0.1.0"
  sysl    = "0.6.0"
}

// **`rp2040blocks` is the chip and `pico` is the board's host.** They are not alternatives: the first
// drives the RP2040's own peripherals and needs no C at all, the second is the pico-sdk's surface
// reached through `extern`. Everything this board hangs off its GPIOs — the two LEDs, the two
// buttons, the buzzer, the RGB LED, the joystick on ADC0/ADC1, and the panel's SPI — is the chip's,
// which is `rp2040blocks`. Standard output over USB, the clock, and the wireless chip are the SDK's,
// which is `pico`.
//
// **`rp2040` itself is a transitive dependency now and not a direct one.** It was direct until the
// SPI and GPIO code written here moved out into `rp2040blocks`, which is the whole point of that
// package existing: what is left in this program is the pin *numbers*, and a pin number is the one
// thing that is genuinely about this carrier board.
//
// `st7796` is the panel. It is not tied to this board or to any chip: it is arithmetic on bytes and
// calls to three `*extern` callbacks that this program supplies, which is precisely the split that
// keeps it a driver rather than a board file.
dependencies {
  rp2040blocks { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/rp2040blocks", version = "0.1.1" }
  pico         { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/pico",         version = "0.1.1" }
  st7796       { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/st7796",       version = "0.1.1" }
}

// **`heap = true` because the SDK links newlib's allocator whether or not this program touches
// one**, which is the reason `sh.sysl.pico` gives for the same line. Nothing here allocates yet.
//
// `sh.sysl.plutovg` is not a dependency yet and it will be: it is the rasterizer that turns this
// from a panel that can be filled into a panel that can be drawn on, and the two packages were built
// to pair — plutovg renders an ARGB32 band, st7796 converts it to RGB565 as it pushes. Its
// `requires { posix = true }` turned out not to be the obstacle it looks like — that clause is
// `write_png` and the font loader, and a program depending on plutovg gets past analysis for
// `thumbv6m-freestanding`, which is where a capability refusal would land.
requires {
  heap = true
}
